WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.420
Gemini 3.7 Flash ships with targeted gains in software engineering and knowledge tasks, while carrying an introductory price set at half the original 3.6 Flash cost. The model also runs noticeably faster than prior Flash versions according to Demis Hassebus. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 141. Google released the new Flash model with major upgrades for software engineering, web development, and knowledge work.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:40.880
Demis Hassebus highlighted the speed gains and positioned the release as immediately usable for builders. The move directly addresses the capability gains versus cost trajectory question that was active yesterday.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:56.620
Teams working on coding agents or web tooling should test the new pricing tier first to see where it beats existing options. DeepMind launched SL2T, a sign language to text model that lets users sign directly to phones for the first time.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:09.360
The model was developed in close collaboration with the deaf community. It marks the first time a production grade system supports direct signing input on consumer devices without intermediate translation steps.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:21.600
DeepSeek raised prices on its flagship models by a factor of 4. The move reverses earlier aggressive discounting and will affect cost calculations for teams relying on the open-weight family.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:33.540
Enterprises that had built workflows around the previous pricing must now re-evaluate total inference spend. The change applies across the company's main model lineup and takes effect immediately.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:43.980
A dedicated analysis from latent space frames the Gemini 3.7 Flash launch as bringing Google DeepMind back to the forefront of the frontier model race.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:52.540
The piece examines how the combination of speed, engineering focus and price reduction positions the model against competing offerings.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:02:02.380
Open AI added computer history to the chat GPT desktop app allowing the model to remember activity across apps and websites on the user's computer.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:13.380
The feature builds on the earlier chronicle preview with lower token usage and added privacy controls including timeline view, selective clearing and app or website exclusions.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:23.040
Users on Pro, Business and Enterprise plans can opt in via settings to integrations on Mac with EEA rollout coming later.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:37.620
The system also supports pausing and resuming history collection directly from the menu bar. Open AI released an ultrafast mode powered by cerebrus that reaches up to 750 tokens per second on its most intelligent model.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:46.440
The offering targets real-time voice, customer support, coding, design, financial research and security workflows where latency matters.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:52.960
It is positioned for enterprise use cases that need frontier intelligence without waiting on slower generation.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:59.420
A new timeline view lets users review past work and identify frequent tasks for skill building.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:07.600
Controls allow pausing history, excluding specific apps or sites and clearing partial or full records directly from the menu bar.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:16.220
The implementation reduces overall token consumption compared with the prior chronicle preview while maintaining personalization benefits.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:24.300
To activate computer history, users select the feature under settings to integrations inside the chat GPT desktop app on Mac.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:34.100
The rollout is currently global for Pro, Business and Enterprise tiers with access in the EEA, UK and Switzerland scheduled for the coming weeks.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:43.500
Simon Willison shared a live markdown to SVG renderer that accepts a just URL and renders at high, medium and low thinking levels.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:49.000
The tool exposes how different reasoning budgets affect output quality on the same prompt.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.660
Developers working on visual generation pipelines can test it immediately at the linked demo.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:04:00.340
The renderer also surfaces browser specific rendering differences that affect final output.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:08.000
Willison posted a high quality Pelican riding a bicycle image produced only when the model ran at the highest thinking level.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.940
Lower levels produced visibly weaker results on the same prompt.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:18.580
The example illustrates the practical value of spending extra test time compute on creative visual tasks.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:28.020
Willison also posted direct links to the full transcripts for the high, medium and low thinking runs so others can inspect the exact reasoning traces.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.440
Okay, let's pop the hood on constraint reasoning.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:42.880
Everyone talks about large language models knowing a rule when accuracy rises, yet the actual mechanism is often just surface level pattern matching rather than internal routing of the constraint.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:53.400
In practice, the model encodes the constraint symmetrically, whether the prompt contains it or not, but only sometimes activates that encoding during the final decision step.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:05:03.660
When activation fails, the model defaults to a conservative answer even though the constraint was represented in the hidden states at above 88% probe accuracy.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:14.300
Activation patching can restore the correct behavior in one failure mode, lifting log probability by roughly six knots, while the second mode shows almost no repair.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:24.020
The distinction matters because prompt-based interventions only inflate conservative bias through a single mediation path and never open the blocked routing channel.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:32.600
A quartet of diagnostic tests run across 14 models confirmed two distinct failure modes rather than a single uniform deficit.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:48.980
Teams building agents that must respect implicit feasibility constraints therefore need to measure routing success, not just final accuracy, and should prefer architectures or fine-tuning regimes that strengthen the activation step rather than adding more surface instructions.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:57.580
The same pattern appears in both open weight and closed models, indicating the bottleneck is architectural rather than training data specific.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:05.800
When the constraint is present but not routed, downstream repair via donor activation succeeds in only one of the two observed modes.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.460
So when should you actually reach for this versus the alternative?

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:18.000
Run the four-conditioned diagnostic first to determine whether accuracy shortfalls stem from missing knowledge or from failed activation.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:27.880
Test Gemini 3.7 Flash on a current coding or web-dev task to see whether the halved price and speed gains change your daily modded choice.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:37.620
Enable computer history in the chat GPT desktop app if you work across multiple sites and want the model to retain context without repeated explanations.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:47.980
Run Simon Willison's SVG renderer on a visual generation prompt at high versus medium thinking levels to observe the quality jump for yourself.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:58.460
Compare the new ultra-fast cerebris backed endpoint against your current inference setup on a latency-sensitive workflow such as real-time support or code completion.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:07.620
Further regional rollout of chat GPT computer history to EEA, UK, and Switzerland users is expected in the coming weeks.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:16.420
Continued price and speed experiments across frontier flash-class models will likely continue as labs respond to the recent Gemini release.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:24.580
More sign language and accessibility multimodal releases may follow the SL2T pattern of community collaborative training.

